Part 1
Getting Ideas
3This page intentionally left blank
3Chapter 1
Creativity and general problem 
solving
There are many Thi ngs that a writer cannot control in ­ indus-
try. What we can control are the ideas we choose for our scripts. The most 
important ingredient in screenwriting is a first-rate film idea. This may sound obvious, but in fact many very talented writers do not fulfil their potential to write world-class scripts ży choose ideas which, while interesting and deeply felt, unfortunately, are simply not strikingly original enough æ highly competitive, expensive medium of feature film, particularly now that feature film ạ compete for market share with drama from an ever-increasing number of platforms. Of course, we should all be passionately committed to ­s we are writing, but ī no point in writing a script that has no chance of being made, and this means asking the hard questions, and asking them early. The question to ask ñ so much ‘Do 
i 
passionately 
want to write ů on this topic?’ but rather, ‘ is m
y film so vividly original 
that Ás all over the world will go ¯ cinema on a wet mon
day night 
to see it?’ (û particularly important for art-house films, ğy already ł limited demographic.) 
if i
t ñ, ask yourself whether you want to 
invest a couple of years of your life in a script only to have producers tell you that you’re a terrific writer but unfortunately your script won’t find a market. 
all
 of us, experienced writers and newcomers, ö take on competi-
tion § way that Olympic athletes do: calmly, consciously and consistently aiming high, pushing our imagination and talent ¯ limits. 
in 
practical 
terms, we can try to do this by employing targeted brainstorming ű good ideğn meticulously planning our script structure. 
Getting good ideas
The two perennial writing problems in scriptwriting are getting good ideas and structuring them properly. The enemy is pressure, particularly pressure of time, because it triggers a panic reflex that tempts us either to use clichés or to write story material that strains credibility.
The fact that so many clichéd and unbelievable films are made every year 
