is testament ¯ power of this panic reflex. it w ould be reassuring if Đ 
these weak films were made by inferior talents, but the sobering reality is 
Ã people creating and funding such films—the people seduced and dazzled by what will appear ¯ rest £ world and, eventually, ¯m-selves as a second-rate movie—are usually highly talented, sophisticated and commercially savvy people â string of successful credits; otherwise they would not be § position of making films in œ place. The lesson ù pressure hğ power to warp the judgment £ best of us. We can all make stupid mistakes. Unfortunately, as we can’t slow the industry down, the pressure ñ going to go away. Time pressures have been ư the dramatist’s job description since at least 
sha
kespeare’s day, ´ reality for 
scriptwriters is Ã job ñ just about talent but about a capacity to be talented on command and to a deadline. 
so 
what can we do? Well, we can ignore the problem and hope that it will 
never ē to us. a m
ore sensible choice is to realise that at some point it 
will, then attempt to define and pinpoint what is going on intellectually when we make stress-related writing mistakes, and also when we are writing well. That way, not only should we be able to short-circuit the stress-related impulses towards cliché ´ incredible, but hopefully—if we can just work out what’s occurring mentally when we are writing well—we may be able to reproduce the process to order, and find techniques that will help us to be creative at speed, under pressure and for long periods of time. 
