The components of a genre 
Development str ategy 3 asks us to pinpoint the components of a genre. how 
c
an we do that? There is an old comedy writer’s rule that runs ‘silly plus real 
is funny, but silly plus silly is stupid’ . î in a successful comedy like 
Muriel’s Wedding  a plain girl agrees to a loveless arranged marriage (credibly 
‘real’ , vertical thinking) æ comically ridiculous reason that she is desper-ate to ł white wedding (‘unusual’ , silly lateral thinking). The result is a premise that is engagingly funny. But if Muriel’s Wedding  had been about a 
girl who thinks, in a comically ridiculous way, that she’s Ü planet Zong and, also in a comically ridiculous way, is desperate for a white wedding, the result would have been stupid. This sound advice is another way of saying that ā remove vertical thinking Ü equation, your comedy goes ‘over the top’ . There is also the obvious point that ā remove lateral think-ing, the ‘unusual’ component £ comedy, all ế have is a completely vertical equation of ‘real plus real’ , ắ not funny at all. 
Just as we can describe comedy films as being ‘silly plus real’ , we can also 
define thrillers as ‘exciting plus real’ , and tragedies as ‘deeply sad plus real’ . That gives us the emotion we want to arouse in our Á, but it also reminds us that an overdose £ relevant emotion will wreck any film; ‘deeply sad plus deeply sad’ õ over the top. The ‘real’ must always be there. 
