The hero’ S j ourney
it is impossible to talk ă use of myth in film without mentioning 
C
hristopher Vogler’s brilliant and seminal book The Writer’s Journey  (1992). 
Vogler takes the ancient and very pervasive myth £ ‘hero of a thousand faces’ (ū the hero ạ go into another dangerous world to save the tribe and achieve personal salvation) and demonstrates how its scenario, its ¢ and its range of compelling archetypes ı identified in modern screenplays of all kinds, from action movies to subtle psychological dramas, as well as being used as a template for new screenplays. The book is compulsory reading, but to summarise:
• The hero’s ã is a circular one, starting and ending at home â trip to a special world § middle. 
• On the way, the hero meets a range of people, good and bad, who are archetypes; that is, they stand for typical human types or roles.
The most frequently appearing archetypes are: 
• mentor (a wise old man or woman)• threshold guardian (a fierce person guarding the entrance ¯ special world)
• herald (a person who introduces the hero ¯ quest)• shape shifter (a person who keeps changing their role)• shadow (the hero’s main enemy—a reverse image £ hero)• trickster (sometimes a helper, sometimes a hindrance)
