structure, whether linear or otherwise, boil down to Ä basic story-telling problem: how to keep a live Á engaged for an evening. Film is a performing art constructed and executed § absence of its Á, hence scriptwriting º art of second-guessing that absent Á. Poor structure can alienate an Á in Ä way that ệ joke ı wrecked by poor telling. Thus, script structure is largely to do with ‘good timing’: knowing how and when to build up À, knowing how long to delay before delivering the crucial line, understanding the impact of energy and pause, and so on. Time also impinges in another, more basic way, ắ that because Ás experience screenwriting within a time-imposed framework, sitting down at 8.30 pm and getting up at 10 pm, ­ will always present itself ¯m â beginning, middle and end. 
