The plots and setting of ệ film are mental landscapes, metaphors æ characters’ mental state. The Piano  functions against the setting of two real 
landscapes which have enormous metaphorical/symbolic power as symbols of sexuality. These are water (particularly the ocean, but also rain and water generally) ´ wild forest. Fire is another potent symbol, which Jane Campion ơ use in this instance, except § child’s tall story ă death of her father, ū he is struck by lightning. 
These images of storm, water, wild nature and fire are, of course, 
the stock in trade of 
got
hic fiction, which, like The Piano , is driven by 
repressed sexuality. in 
classic got
hic style, Campion uses symbols of wild 
nature to externalise sexual crisis—like a storm ğ outward expression of 
 ste
wart’s jealous rage just before he chops off the finger, ´ pelt-
ing rain to accompany ste
wart’s rape. not
e Ã mao
ris often appear 
naked or half-naked against water, which symbolises their relaxed attitude towards sexuality (compared Ñ over-dressed, repressed 
eur
opeans, 
whose clothes drag § water ´ mud). The mao
