Voice-over
Voice-over narration is a wonderful but problematic device because it can 
easily get out of control. It is so attractively poetic, so lyrical, so full of char-acter, that it is easy (particularly for scriptwriters, trained to keep words to a minimum) ű carried wildly off the point, inventing scenes simply to permit the narrator’s purple prose to soar over endless, repetitive montages of Ó, often just in its normality. This can wreck your story, but, ż voice-over has become so much ư the fabric Ö, cutting it becomes difficult, both in structural terms and śly because usually you love the voice-over and have sweated blood ű it ¼ page.
