adaptation of fiction
Many fine films have been adaptations of novels. Not surprisingly then, 
studios and independent producers often actively seek out novels for adap-tation, assuming that a successful novel will automatically translate into a successful film. Unfortunately, this frequently isn’t the case, not only because some novels are too long or too internal for film, but żre is a funda-mentally different contract between writer and Á in film and fiction. The difference is this: for Ás, fiction is ă ã, ý ă destination. The same people who happily tell you Ãir favourite novel ‘takes fifty pages ű going’ will, if sitting in a cinema, get angry with slow scenes in seconds. We love novels that go off the point for a chapter to examine Ó’s bizarre but utterly irrelevant 
aun
tie Nellie. do 
this 
in ů and ø will get actively hostile. Many novels have very little plot and end in mid air, in a deliberate anti¿. You just can’t do that in feature film. The film Á demands from very early that ț has a story consisting of a chain-reaction of events and a ‘point’—even if that point, as in Mulholland Drive or Caché (Hidden ), is to question ¦ 
conventions, including the notion of ‘point’ in itself. 
Film needs to engage immediately and rise steadily and incrementally to a 
¿, but many novels, as well as starting slowly and ending in anti¿, are episodic. Often, the only way film can insert the vital rising À into epi-sodic story material is by using a parallel ¦ form, for example ¬. This permits the episodes to be quarantined in ę while a strong story » pulls ­ forwards, thus preventing slowing. This will give you ệ result, but it raises serious issues about staying true ¯ book. To make ­ £ book ř pulled the book apart.
