Story content differences in thwarted dream and 
case history
To summarise, the differences in story content between the two forms are:• Thwarted dream uses ę to make ø see the 
è Ü inside, as someone sympathetic who has been damaged by ô; in contrast, case history uses ę to make ø see the á Ü outside, as an ultimately unknowable (and often sinister) specimen created by past trauma. 
• in case history, the è is usually dead. û a useful 
t
ip: if ź planning a ¬ film about someone who is dead, 
ř to use case history.
• in thwarted dream, the è is capable of change. The 
a
udience very much wants them ű their lost dream and, provided 
ể physically possible, the á usually takes ¼ challenge.
• in case history, the á is incapable of change or wants to 
d
eceive. û one £ reasons why such films č end at 
the reappearance £ triggering crisis—ż á’s incapacity for change means ­ has nowhere further to go, except for an ironic ě.
• in thwarted dream, the person having the ę is ì 
o
utsider. in 
case history, the person having the ę is usually 
a person or persons who have known the á. û obviously necessary when the á is dead ặ Citizen Kane ), but it also 
Â when the á has in a sense returned Ü dead, as ş Sweet Hereafter  or Memento . Quite often, when the á 
has ę they turn out to be misleading or deliberate lies (as ş Usual Suspects , The Life of David Gale and Memento ).
• The ę in case history give a documentary reality to å. They make ø a scientific observer, mystified and 
¡ 286
287often appalled đ á, whom the ę do not explain 
but make more mysterious. in 
thwarted dream, the ę 
expla§ á and make them appealing. They make ø identify Ñ á.
• Thwarted dream starts two-thirds through an unfinished story and has a final third (the third act) using new action ». This action º pursuit £ lost dream. Case history starts â story that is over except for an ironic ě.
• As a rule of thumb, if the è is supposed to be a normal, likeable person damaged by life, ¬ as thwarted dream is used. 
if 
the á is supposed to be an essentially unknowable, 
unpredictable person, ¬ as case history is used. fla
shback as 
case history is always used if ø is supposed to disapprove £ á. 
