Consecutive stories ¦: An 
introduction
Consecutive stories form describes films as different as Rashomon , Pulp Fic­
tion, City of God , The Joy Luck Club , Amores Perros , Run Lola Run , Paris, je 
t’aime , Night on Earth , Coffee and Cigarettes , Atonement , The Circle, Button­
ers, Ten, Go! and, less successfully,  Vantage Point. 
All £se films tell a series of separate stories (often different versions 
or consequences of Ä event) one ề other, linking them ţ 
at š. i u
sed to call this sort of structure ‘sequential ¦’ , but that 
caused confusion â theory about constructing ō ¦ in sequences. 
i n
ow use the term ‘consecutive stories’ , which, if clumsy, is at least 
descriptive and impossible to confuse ânything else. 
Sometimes these films are described as ‘composite films’ , ắ not a 
useful term because it is often taken to include « and Î films, which present very different writing problems from consecutive-stories films. Whereas « and Î films jump between simultaneously running stories, consecutive stories films tell semi-complete, sometimes even complete stories one ề after, creating a connection at š. û an utterly different plotting task. 
Consecutive stories is one £ hardest parallel ¦ forms to 
