script as an instruction manual
as Well as a piece of drama, a script in all its forms (including treatment, 
o
utline, beat sheet and scene breakdown) is a technical instruction manual 
for everyone involved § process of creating ­. This means it has 
to be written and laid out according to industry conventions. The scripts § previous chapters have been laid out differently for ease of reading in a book format. 
acting and camera directions
Unō acting and camera directions should not matter, but they do. 
scr
ipt layout guides with full glossaries of camera terms are available from 
organisations such ğ Writers’ gui
ld in your country. lim
its of space pre-
vent their inclusion here.
Writing well æ camera means transmitting information about what is 
appearing on screen in an economical, vivid but unobtrusive way. Putting in a lot of acting and camera directions is called ‘directing on paper’ and actors and directors hate it, partly because it pre-empts their input, partly because it interrupts the flow £ script, making it impossible to read for flow and running time. The script should take Ä time to read as it takes to play ¼ screen and should play § mind’s eye in Ä order as it would on screen. Don’t make readers rewind. 
nev
er put any information in direc-
tions that is obvious from å or dialogue. 
