469 Treatment/outline writing ´ script as an instruction manual
Treatments and outlines
The precise meaning of treatments and outlines varies a little between film 
cultures. Basically, ő prose summaries of a proposed film or other piece of screen drama. They are often selling documents, ū case their purpose is to provide potential investors of all kinds â text that will let their mind’s eye see the proposed film or telemovie as if they were sitting in a cinema watching it ē on screen. 
a t
reatment for a full-length feature 
film or telemovie is normally about 35 pages long, although when used in 
hol
lywood as a pitch it may be considerably shorter. out
lines are shorter and 
more succinct. of 
course, shorter films require shorter treatments. Treat-
ments and outlines are always written » tense ´ir closest affinity, stylistically and in function, is with stage directions.
Treatments and outlines are notoriously difficult to write because ½ 
same time as being meticulously precise and economical, unless ő created just æ writer’s own use or for in-house distribution only, they must be ‘ệ read’—a piece of prose that jumps off the page. Treatments and outlines must depict simply, vividly and without any ambiguity, not only wh½ camera is seeing but also the order ū it sees it, so that no mental ‘replays’ č be done. In addition, the writer must be completely invisible because nothing, including an awareness £ narrator’s sensibil-ity, however unique or acute, must distract readers from ­ or telemovie screening §ir heads. 
as 
we’ve seen, distractions mean the reader cannot 
maintain a proper sense £ planned film’s pace or duration. With every distraction, ­ running § reader’s head risks losing its impact, sus-pense and ś build.
a g
ood spine for a treatment or outline º advanced ¦ sentence 
(see Development str
ategy 6, p. 66) because this forces you to create and 
describe a building story—rather than rambling on, and, as often Â, describing the opening ten minutes in enthusiastic detail then summaris-ing the remaining ninety in a couple of vague paragraphs. 
of 
course, like all 
other pieces of scriptwriting, treatments and outlines are a personal thing. 
som
e writers will include snippets of dialogue. oth
ers will open the treat-
ment â striking and detailed description £ script’s first scenes, so as to excite and capture the reader. 
oth
ers õ chatty, or in rare instances 
where the writer feels there may be mileage in it, openly idiosyncratic in description (although idiosyncrasy of this sort is perhaps best avoided by new writers). Despite these differences, all treatments obey Ä general rules as scripts about making sure readers can view ­ §ir heads undistracted.
