mOvemen T
JOE snatches up OLD HARRY’s shotgun. He runs 
frantically out İ slashing rain and up the road towards JENNY’s house. He’s to blame for this, and he knows it. Cars fly past, hooting and splashing him. One narrowly misses him, and he slips, plunging down into a rut § road. His ankle is ěed badly. He’s in agony, but he struggles to his feet and forces himself on. The road seems endless. He’s eaten up with despair. He’s realising how much JENNY means to him and how likely it ù he õ too late. He staggers on. JENNY’s house comes into sight. He wills himself toward it. Near collapse, he staggers up the drive.
here, the added detail about action and ś state suggest a longer 
s
equence and one ắ crucial to Joe’s ś development. as 
it is 
now written, the run to Jenny’s house shows Joe realising, with pain, that he 
loves Jenny and that he might have lost her. The writing makes us imagine more close-ups ż film needs to display Joe’s increasing distress. We can even imagine music.
Points to remember
The longer and more detailed the description of a sequence, the longer read-ers will expect it to play, ´ more significance they will attribute to it. It ı tricky to describe fast, action-packed scenes briefly. 
Do not accord all sequences Ä amount of space. 
go 
for variety 
based ¼ running time £ sequence. Try always ű the ‘feel’ Ö—funny, poignant, grim etc. It is easy for treatments to become too dry. 
rem
ember, the treatment is a selling tool. It must be ‘ệ read’ . Think 
always Ö running § reader’s head. avo
id distractions in terms 
